INT. ABANDONED DRIVE-IN - PROJECTION TOWER - DUSK

A concrete tower at the back of a cracked lot, its screen peeling to bare plywood. Weeds climb through the speaker posts. Dusk throws long orange bars through the broken shutters, and the air smells of old popcorn oil and rust.

Four kids crowd the single room. A battered projector squats on a table, its lens clouded. Reels of film stand stacked like cheese wheels. A folding chair, a cooler, a radio playing low.

CASSIUS "CASS" MWANGI, 17, lanky, a hand-me-down varsity jacket two sizes too small, sits cross-legged on the cooler, picking at a loose thread. He is the one who found the place and he knows it.

FLORENCIA "FLO" RUIZ-ABERNATHY, 16, braids tied up in a bandanna, perches on the table's edge swinging her boots. She watches Cass the way one watches a campfire that might spread.

HENRIK SOLBERG, 17, glasses taped at the hinge, kneels over the projector with a screwdriver, fully absorbed. He hasn't spoken in ten minutes.

And in the corner, half in shadow, PILAR TRAN, 15, hugs her knees and says nothing, a camera hanging from her neck.

                    FLO
You said it'd work.

                    CASS
I said it might.

                    FLO
You said it would.

                    CASS
It might work. It's a hundred years old.

                    HENRIK
It's from 1974.

                    CASS
Same thing.

                    HENRIK
(without looking up)
It is emphatically not the same thing.

Pilar lifts her camera. Clicks. The shutter is loud in the small room. Cass flinches.

                    CASS
Don't.

                    PILAR
It's good light.

                    CASS
I look stupid.

                    PILAR
You look like yourself.

                    CASS
That's what I mean.

Flo snorts. Henrik snaps a panel shut and rises, wiping his hands.

                    HENRIK
Try it.

Cass slides off the cooler. Crosses to the projector. His hand hovers over the switch.

                    CASS
What if it's just a blank screen?

                    FLO
Then we've wasted a Friday.

                    CASS
We've wasted a lot of Fridays.

                    FLO
So one more won't matter.

A beat. He throws the switch. The projector shudders, coughs, and a rectangle of white fire leaps from the lens across the lot, striking the peeling screen.

Nothing. Just white. Then a flicker. A number counts down: 5, 4, 3.

They hold their breath. Henrik, Flo, Pilar, all drawn to the beam.

                    HENRIK
It's working.

The reel catches. The screen shows a black-and-white shot of a woman on a porch, lit from behind, smiling at someone off camera. She holds the smile a long time. Then she turns and looks directly into the lens.

                    PILAR
(barely audible)
Who is that?

                    CASS
It was on the shelf. It had your name on it.

                    PILAR
It did not.

                    CASS
It said TRAN.

                    PILAR
There are a lot of Trans.

                    CASS
In Barlow? There's one.

Pilar stands. Her camera swings from its strap. On the screen the woman mouths something, long and slow. Pilar steps closer to the beam, enormous shadows flung across the lot.

                    FLO
Pilar?

                    PILAR
She's my grandmother.

                    HENRIK
You said your grandmother lived in Hanoi.

                    PILAR
She did.

                    HENRIK
So how did she end up on a porch in Ohio in 1974?

                    PILAR
I don't know.

                    CASS
I thought it'd be nice. A surprise.

                    PILAR
You thought.

                    CASS
I thought--

                    PILAR
You didn't ask me.

                    CASS
It was supposed to be a gift.

                    PILAR
It's my family, Cass! It's mine!

Her voice cracks off the concrete. The film keeps rolling: the woman on the porch, laughing now, silent and radiant.

Flo moves to Pilar's side but doesn't touch her. Henrik switches off the radio. In the stillness the projector chatters.

                    FLO
(softly)
Let her watch.

Cass backs away until his shoulders hit the wall. He stares at the floor. Pilar stands in the light, shadow thrown huge across the screen, the grandmother's face drifting across her own.

She raises the camera. Takes a single photograph of the screen.

Then she lowers it and sits down on the floor, and the others, without a word, settle around her.
